-- CONTROL for `s8-xmod-reexport-alias-constrained`: BYTE-IDENTICAL but for the
-- importer SPELLING (SELECTIVE `lib.rsel.{f}` / bare `f 1`, not `as R` / `R.f 1`).
--
-- GROUND TRUTH, HAND-DERIVED IDENTICALLY: `f x = x + 1` instantiated at `Int`,
-- so `f 1` = 2.
--
-- THIS CONTROL DOES TWO JOBS, and the second CHANGED MEANING when the dict-arity
-- seam was repaired (RUN-XMOD-062):
--   1. It localises an alias-arm regression, exactly as the unconstrained pair's
--      control does.
--   2. It was the ATTRIBUTION for the sibling's REJECTING `run` cell. Both
--      spellings panicked `E-PANIC: intToString: not an Int` on `run` while both
--      were correct on `build`, and that identity was the argument that the run
--      residue was the #1369/#1425 dict-arity seam rather than anything about
--      the ALIAS spelling.
--
-- THE TWO SPELLINGS AGREE AGAIN, AND THAT IDENTITY IS THE ASSERTION -- but only
-- after a detour worth recording. RUN-XMOD-062 made the dict-arity seam's
-- definer rows carry the TRUE DEFINER of a re-exported name (`lib.decl`) rather
-- than the module the importer SPELLS (`lib.rsel`), which repaired THIS
-- spelling while the alias sibling went on panicking. The paragraph that stood
-- here then attributed the survivor to `aliasEntriesFor` copying qual entries
-- keyed on the spelled module. #2840 instrumented that claim and REFUTED it:
-- `aliasEntriesFor` had already been resolving through `graphPubDefiners`, and
-- what the alias route was actually missing was its entry in the DICT-NAME set
-- (`aliasDictNamesOfPath`), which asked what the spelled module DEFINES -- and a
-- pure re-exporter defines nothing. With that arm reading the graph too, both
-- spellings print `2` at exit 0 on every verb.
--
-- ⇒ Read this pair by WHICH MEMBER REDS: this row alone means the named-form
-- definer resolution regressed; both means the shared route did.
import lib.rsel.{f}

main = println (f 1)
